{"compatibility":"minor","safety_note_md":"# SafeStan Safety Note

Compatibility: minor.

Protected data: `Y_safestan`.

Generated files: `model_safe.stan`, `data_safe.json`.

Changes: replaced the explicit manual `target +=` multinomial/Bernoulli log-probability terms with one built-in vectorized `bernoulli_logit` sampling statement. Added `Y_safestan`, with deterministic relation `Y_safestan[i] = 1` for every `i = 1, ..., I`; this is the Bernoulli/category indicator corresponding to the original transformed-data row `Y[i] = [1, 0]`. The logit is `beta * (est[i, 1] - est[i, 2])`, so `bernoulli_logit_lpmf(1 | ...)` is exactly the original `log(p[i, 1])` contribution. The deterministic construction of `est` was rewritten as a per-row conditional fill from the same context inputs; no protected data are read before their sampling statement.
","model_safe_stan":"/*
 * Endo: conditional inference in case-contrl studies 
 * http://www.openbugs.net/Examples/Endo.html
 *
 * In this example, three methods of different 
 * model specifications are used for one equivalent
 * model. This is method 2. 

 * FIXME: using the multinomial specification  
 */
data {
  int n10;
  int n01;
  int n11;
  int I;
  int J;
  array[I] int<lower=0, upper=1> Y_safestan;
}
transformed data {
  array[I, 2] int<lower=0> est;
  for (i in 1 : I) {
    if (i <= n10) {
      est[i, 1] = 1;
      est[i, 2] = 0;
    } else if (i <= n10 + n01) {
      est[i, 1] = 0;
      est[i, 2] = 1;
    } else if (i <= n10 + n01 + n11) {
      est[i, 1] = 1;
      est[i, 2] = 1;
    } else {
      est[i, 1] = 0;
      est[i, 2] = 0;
    }
  }
}
parameters {
  real beta;
}
model {
  vector[I] eta;

  // METHOD 2 - conditional likelihoods
  beta ~ normal(0, 1000);

  for (i in 1 : I) {
    eta[i] = beta * (est[i, 1] - est[i, 2]);
  }
  Y_safestan ~ bernoulli_logit(eta);
}
","data_safe_json":"{"I":4,"J":2,"n01":4,"n10":5,"n11":5,"Y_safestan":[1,1,1,1]}","protected_variables":["Y_safestan"]}
